{
  "about": "\"Link your phone\" (the web chat's Accounts screen, Xavier 2026-11-19): a QR of https://chat.<domain>/login/<username> that the iPhone or Android app scans (or the phone's camera opens as a universal / app link) to log in as <username>@<domain> on that phone. Nothing secret is in the link: the app starts the ordinary e-mail-code sign-in for that address at once and shows \"Please confirm the 6-digit code sent to your e-mail\". A login link is exactly: scheme https; host chat.<domain> where <domain> is a hostname (the host is read in lower case); path /login/<username> and nothing after it (one trailing slash is fine); no query, no fragment, no user or port; <username> by the server's rule: lower-case letters and digits, with . _ - allowed inside (not first, not last), 1 to 30 characters, written in lower case. Anything else is not a link we know: the scanner says so and nothing is asked of any server. address = <username>@<domain>.",
